# Dockerfile Architecture

The Dockerfile is based on the following pipeline:

```text
RESTORE
   |
   v
 BUILD
   |
   +--------> TEST
   |
   v
 PUBLISH
   |
   v
 RUNTIME
```

Each stage has a specific responsibility.

This approach prevents unnecessary development tools and source files from being included in the final production image.

---

# 1. Restore Stage

The restore stage uses the .NET SDK image:

```dockerfile
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:${DOTNET_VERSION} AS restore
```

The SDK image is required because restoring NuGet dependencies requires the .NET SDK.

The working directory is configured with:

```dockerfile
WORKDIR /src
```

Only the solution and project files are copied first.

Example:

```dockerfile
COPY Dungeon.Domain/Dungeon.Domain.csproj \
     Dungeon.Domain/
```

This is important for Docker caching.

If application source code changes but the `.csproj` files remain unchanged, Docker can reuse the existing NuGet restore layer.

This avoids downloading all dependencies again during every build.

---

# NuGet Cache

The restore command uses Docker BuildKit:

```dockerfile
RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.nuget/packages \
    dotnet restore Dungeon.Presentation.slnx
```

The NuGet package directory is cached between builds.

This improves build performance, especially in CI/CD environments.

The cache directory is:

```text
/root/.nuget/packages
```

---

# 2. Build Stage

The build stage starts from the restored image:

```dockerfile
FROM restore AS build
```

The complete source code is then copied:

```dockerfile
COPY . .
```

The solution is compiled in Release mode:

```dockerfile
dotnet build Dungeon.Presentation.slnx \
    --configuration Release \
    --no-restore
```

The `--no-restore` option is used because dependencies were already restored during the previous stage.

This prevents unnecessary operations and makes the build faster.

---

# 3. Test Stage

The test stage executes the automated tests:

```dockerfile
FROM build AS test
```

The test project is executed with:

```dockerfile
dotnet test Dungeon.Test/Dungeon.Test.csproj \
    --configuration Release \
    --no-build \
    --verbosity normal \
    --logger "console;verbosity=normal"
```

The `--no-build` option prevents rebuilding the solution because it was already compiled during the build stage.

This stage can be used independently in CI/CD.

Example:

```bash
docker build --target test -t dungeon-test .
```

If a test fails, Docker returns a non-zero exit code.

This allows a CI/CD pipeline to stop the deployment automatically.

---

# 4. Publish Stage

The publish stage generates the final production application files.

```dockerfile
FROM build AS publish
```

The application is published using:

```dockerfile
dotnet publish Dungeon.Presentation/Dungeon.Presentation.csproj \
    --configuration Release \
    --output /app/publish \
    --no-build \
    --no-restore \
    /p:UseAppHost=false
```

The published application is stored in:

```text
/app/publish
```

The following option is used:

```text
/p:UseAppHost=false
```

This prevents .NET from generating an unnecessary platform-specific executable.

The application will instead be launched using:

```bash
dotnet Dungeon.Presentation.dll
```

---

# 5. Runtime Stage

The final stage uses the ASP.NET Core runtime image:

```dockerfile
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:${DOTNET_VERSION} AS runtime
```

The .NET SDK is not included in the production image.

This reduces:

- Image size
- Attack surface
- Number of installed components
- Potential vulnerabilities

Only the ASP.NET Core runtime required to execute the application is present.

---

# Runtime Working Directory

The application runs from:

```dockerfile
WORKDIR /app
```

Published files are copied from the publish stage:

```dockerfile
COPY --from=publish --chown=$APP_UID:$APP_UID /app/publish ./
```

Only production files are transferred to the final image.

The following elements are therefore excluded:

- Source code
- Test projects
- .NET SDK
- Build tools
- Intermediate build files

---

# Environment Variables

The following production environment variables are configured:

```dockerfile
ENV ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production \
    ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS=8080 \
    DOTNET_RUNNING_IN_CONTAINER=true \
    DOTNET_EnableDiagnostics=0
```

## ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT

```text
ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production
```

This configures ASP.NET Core to use the production environment.

It may affect:

- Logging
- Error handling
- Configuration loading
- Middleware behavior

---

## ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS

```text
ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS=8080
```

The ASP.NET Core application listens on TCP port:

```text
8080
```

---

## DOTNET_RUNNING_IN_CONTAINER

```text
DOTNET_RUNNING_IN_CONTAINER=true
```

This explicitly indicates that the application is running inside a container environment.

---

## DOTNET_EnableDiagnostics

```text
DOTNET_EnableDiagnostics=0
```

.NET diagnostic capabilities are disabled inside the production container.

This reduces unnecessary runtime capabilities and slightly improves container hardening.

Diagnostics should only be enabled when required for troubleshooting or observability tooling.

---

# Network Port

The application exposes port:

```dockerfile
EXPOSE 8080
```

The container listens internally on:

```text
8080
```

Example local execution:

```bash
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 dungeon-api
```

The application can then be accessed through:

```text
http://localhost:8080
```

---

# Non-Root Execution

The container does not run as root.

The final Dockerfile contains:

```dockerfile
USER $APP_UID
```

Modern ASP.NET Core container images include an unprivileged application user.

Running the application as a non-root user limits the potential impact of a compromised application.

The application files are also copied with the appropriate ownership:

```dockerfile
COPY --from=publish --chown=$APP_UID:$APP_UID /app/publish ./
```

This ensures that the runtime user owns the application files.

---

# Application Entry Point

The microservice is started using:

```dockerfile
ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "Dungeon.Presentation.dll"]
```

The final command executed by the container is therefore:

```bash
dotnet Dungeon.Presentation.dll
```

